     "text": "\u0007The student warms the product from (c)(i) and tests any gas given off.\nobservations ........................................................................................................................\n.......................................................................................................................................  [1]\nTests on solid D\n\u0007Table 3.1 shows the tests and the student’s observations for solid D.\ntests\nobservations\ntest 1\nDo a flame test on solid D.\nyellow coloured flame\ntest 2\nGently heat about half of the remaining solid D.\nsteam is given off and condensation\nforms at the top of the boiling tube\nHold a strip of anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride\nthe anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride\npaper at the mouth of the boiling tube.\npaper changes colour\ntest 3\nDissolve the remaining solid D in water to form\nsolution D.\nDivide solution D into three portions.\nTo the first portion of solution D, add aqueous\ngreen precipitate which is insoluble in excess\nammonia dropwise until in excess.\ntest 4\nTo the second portion of solution D, add a piece\ngreen precipitate\nof aluminium foil and about 5 cm3 of aqueous\nsodium hydroxide.\nHeat the mixture formed and hold damp red\nthe red litmus paper remains red\nlitmus paper at the mouth of the boiling tube.\ntest 5\nTo the third portion of solution D, add about\neffervescence\n5 cm3 of dilute nitric acid.\nBubble any gas formed through limewater.\nthe limewater becomes milky",
